---
/**
 * The privacy page, and the only one.
 *
 * It lives at /privacy/ rather than under /docs/ because that is the address a
 * store listing, a repository and this site's own header already point at, and
 * it is listed in the documentation navigation like any other page. It covers
 * the app and this website, because splitting them produced two pages that
 * each said half of the same thing.
 *
 * Every claim here is checked against the app's source by tools/verify-facts.mjs.
 * If you change a sentence about behaviour, change the check with it.
 */
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { page } from '../data/docs-nav';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../data/site';

const entry = page('/privacy/');
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <h2>Nothing leaves by default</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Hozz sends Health data only to destinations you add and confirm.</strong> There is no
    hosted relay or default destination.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Each destination receives only its selected Health types and runs at its chosen cadence.</li>
    <li>Connection tests contain a fixed probe, not Health data.</li>
    <li>Mac discovery uses Bonjour and local-subnet probes. It stays on your network.</li>
    <li>No analytics, ads, update checks, feature flags or remote crash reports make requests.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Credentials stay on device</h2>

  <p>
    Destination tokens, API keys and MQTT passwords use the device Keychain setting{' '}
    <code>kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly</code>. They do not sync or enter backups.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Mac pairing token is the exception: <code>kSecAttrSynchronizable</code> shares it through
    your iCloud Keychain, inside an access group only Hozz apps can use. Without shared iCloud
    Keychain, pairing happens over the local network.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="MQTT usernames are settings, not secrets">
    <p>The username is stored in the app database. Only the password uses the Keychain.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Storage</h2>

  <p>
    The phone stores cursors, coverage, destination settings, receipts and staged batches—not a
    permanent Health mirror. Its database uses <code>completeUnlessOpen</code> file protection and is
    excluded from backups.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Mac receiver keeps a durable SQLite copy and excludes it from backups. macOS has no matching
    per-file protection class, so enable FileVault to protect it at rest.
  </p>

  <h2>Logs omit data and secrets</h2>

  <p>
    Unified logs contain status and failure descriptions, never sample values, credentials or
    destination secrets. The Mac may log its own local addresses during pairing. Rejected response
    bodies are discarded because they may echo a Health record.
  </p>

  <h2>Health access is read-only</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz requests read access when you first export or sync. It never requests write access and never
    writes to Apple Health. <a href="/docs/data-coverage/#what-hozz-will-never-do">Why not.</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Control ends at your destination</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Public endpoints should use TLS and authentication.</li>
    <li>A cloud assistant may upload readings it requests through <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP</a>.</li>
    <li>A synced folder follows that provider's rules.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>This website</h2>

  <p>
    Static files on Cloudflare Pages. No cookies, analytics, storage or third-party resources.
    Cloudflare keeps host logs under its policy; external links use the destination site's policy.
  </p>

  <p><a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener">Inspect the source and tests.</a></p>
</DocsLayout>
